1115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511430 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511430 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511430 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511430 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d65b68cd-44fe-525c-bfeb-caea3373b3e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511468 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511468 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/393c612a7ecb682e28bf9de46024a3681e8f6e58a9add78c0b24a870aa00fa7a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,4]$ given that $f(0)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b07da73-507b-5a7d-831e-9986becac517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511468 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-1 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+(-8) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,-9\\}=-9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-9,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511469 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511469 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511469 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511469 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/31/3123021ea5734c0ff2e8edcb716245b603de6f9dde7ebf0d367e1eeb8383bdf8.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $10$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,0]$ given that $f(-6)=2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511469 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20a7e745-4621-5ebb-935e-e596b9f08fba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511469 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-3,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6) & =2 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+3-10 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,5,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511487 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511487 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/db/dbbbcdb54dc8d6f96957ca01f4eeccea0c24c79eb95fc6c10953534d38e4d724.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-8,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6,$ $2,$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-8,0]$ given that $f(-8)=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4760d6e4-5155-5d78-b6d2-f55eb97036fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511487 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-8,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-8,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-8,-4,-2,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-8) & =2 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6-2 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6-2+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,8,6,10\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511506 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511506 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511506 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/73/73610eebf575c612fc5b64ec6df264baffb338bdf3f2dbdc848477262874a54d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $7$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2]$ given that $f(-3)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511506 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6cbda409-a223-57c5-93ac-3f78fe61c842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511506 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+4 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+4-7 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{5,9,2\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343853" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511528 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b1/b1d5208f46af834deda70086b9ef4b91de